# Standard library: geometry
#
# Points, distances and angles in the plane, triangles, and congruence.
#
# One record per item. A record begins at column 0 with its kind, axiom,
# theorem or definition, and its name; `mundane` before the kind marks an
# item a proof takes for granted. A proof imports each item it cites,
# `import <header> stdlib/geometry/<name>`, where the header is the
# record's own before its name, as `mundane theorem` or `axiom`. Format is described in DATABASE.md.
#
# An item's statement is written in the theorem form of SYNTAX.md: let and
# assume lines with labels, then a `then` line.
#
# Every item carries at least one of:
#   metamath    a set.mm label or labels supply it
#   open        it is cited but unproved and unbridged
#
# Other fields: target, note, and for the one definition that
# introduces a symbol, symbol and defines.


mundane definition point
  then        A is a point ↔ A ∈ 𝔼²
  metamath    biid, since "A is a point" and A ∈ 𝔼² are both built as A ∈ ℂ
  target      biid
  note        A point is a member of the plane. The plane is ℂ in the kernel,
              which the page never says (GEOMETRY.md).

mundane definition angle
  symbol      ang
  defines     vx vy cc cc0 csn cdif cc cc0 csn cdif
              vy cv vx cv cdiv co clog cfv cim cfv cmpo
  metamath    set.mm writes this function inline in every theorem about it and
              never names it, so the corpus declares the constant
  note        The angle the corpus writes is unsigned: ∠PQR is the absolute
              value of ang applied to the two differences, which lands in
              0 to π and makes mun:stdlib/geometry/angle-symmetric true. ang itself is
              signed, in −π to π, as set.mm has it. GEOMETRY.md weighs the
              seven candidate geometries and takes this one.

              This is the only definition in the corpus that introduces a
              symbol; every other names a word for something set.mm already
              has. See GOALS.md decision 12. Its statement is `defines`, what
              the symbol stands for, and what follows from that is
              mun:stdlib/geometry/angle-real.

mundane theorem angle-real
  let P be a point                                                    (H1)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H2)
  let R be a point                                                    (H3)
  assume P ≠ Q                                                        (H4)
  assume R ≠ Q                                                        (H5)
  then        ∠PQR ∈ ℝ. ∠PQR ≥ 0.
  metamath    gangbnd3, which this corpus proves in
              elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm
  target      gangbnd3
  note        ∠PQR is an absolute value, so it is real and at least 0; what
              makes that a proof is that ang of the two differences is a
              number, which gangrange gives by putting ∠PQR in [0, π].

mundane theorem signed-angle-size
  let P be a point                                                    (H1)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H2)
  let R be a point                                                    (H3)
  then        |∡PQR| = ∠PQR
  metamath    eqid, since ∠PQR is built as the absolute value of ∡PQR
  target      eqid
  note        The angle is the signed angle's size, whichever way it turns.

mundane theorem signed-angle-real
  let P be a point                                                    (H1)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H2)
  let R be a point                                                    (H3)
  assume P ≠ Q                                                        (H4)
  assume R ≠ Q                                                        (H5)
  then        ∡PQR ∈ ℝ. −π < ∡PQR. ∡PQR ≤ π.
  metamath    gsangcl, from angcld
  target      gsangcl

theorem signed-angle-reverse
  let P be a point                                                    (H1)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H2)
  let R be a point                                                    (H3)
  assume P ≠ Q                                                        (H4)
  assume R ≠ Q                                                        (H5)
  assume ∡PQR ≠ π                                                     (H6)
  then        ∡RQP = −∡PQR
  metamath    gsangrev, from logrec
  target      gsangrev
  note        Turned the other way, the signed angle changes sign; at π, a
              straight angle, it is π both ways.

theorem signed-angle-sum
  let P be a point                                                    (H1)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H2)
  let R be a point                                                    (H3)
  assume P ≠ Q                                                        (H4)
  assume Q ≠ R                                                        (H5)
  assume P ≠ R                                                        (H6)
  then        ∡RQP + ∡PRQ + ∡QPR ∈ {−π, π}
  metamath    gang180, from ang180
  target      gang180
  note        The signed angles of a triangle, each turned the same way
              around it, add to π or to −π as the triangle runs
              counterclockwise or clockwise. The three points need only be
              apart: on one line the angles are 0, 0 and π.

mundane theorem triangle-angle-real
  let P be a point                                                    (H1)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H2)
  let R be a point                                                    (H3)
  assume P, Q, R form a triangle                                      (H4)
  then        ∠QPR ∈ ℝ. ∠PQR ∈ ℝ. ∠QRP ∈ ℝ.
  metamath    gtriangp, gtriangq, gtriangr, which this corpus proves in
              elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm from gangbnd3
  target      gtriangp, gtriangq, gtriangr
  note        One target to a sentence. mun:stdlib/geometry/angle-real asks
              that the corner differ from both ends, and the triangle says its
              points differ in one order each, so citing it at a corner takes a
              rotated triangle first; this says each angle is a number from the
              triangle as written.

mundane definition collinear
  open        Incidence is a primitive of the geometry, and the readable layer
              cannot state what it is. In the plane it says P = Q or
              (R − P)/(Q − P) is real, which is true in any order of the three
              points, so the notation's points stand in no order; and
              subtraction takes numbers while P, Q and R are points:
              the sorts that stop |CA| reading as a product stop this too. The
              notation carries a target, so a claim of collinearity elaborates;
              what has no readable statement is the equivalence.
  note        Named by the merge because mun:stdlib/geometry/triangle needs a word for what a
              triangle's vertices are not.

mundane definition triangle
  let P be a point                                                    (H1)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H2)
  let R be a point                                                    (H3)
  then        P, Q, R form a triangle ↔ P ≠ Q and Q ≠ R and P ≠ R and
              not (P, Q, R are collinear)
  metamath    gtridef, which this corpus proves in
              elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm: the notation's target is the three
              inequalities and the quotient it denies, and collinearity is the
              first two one point or the quotient real, which with the points
              distinct is the quotient
  target      gtridef
  note        set.mm has no single notion. "Form a triangle" and "are
              collinear" are notations whose targets make the two sides one
              formula; what collinearity is in words is
              mun:stdlib/geometry/collinear, which this statement does not
              need. That the three are points is the three let lines, not a
              clause of the statement; the sorts carry it.

definition congruent
  let P be a point                                                    (H1)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H2)
  let R be a point                                                    (H3)
  let P′ be a point                                                   (H4)
  let Q′ be a point                                                   (H5)
  let R′ be a point                                                   (H6)
  then        triangle PQR ≅ triangle P′Q′R′ ↔ |PQ| = |P′Q′|, |QR| = |Q′R′|,
              |RP| = |R′P′|, ∠PQR = ∠P′Q′R′, ∠QRP = ∠Q′R′P′, and
              ∠RPQ = ∠R′P′Q′
  metamath    biid, since the notation's target is the six equations
  target      biid
  note        set.mm has no congruence of triangles, and the notation stands
              for the six equations themselves, so the two sides are one
              formula.

mundane definition isosceles
  let P be a point                                                    (H1)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H2)
  let R be a point                                                    (H3)
  then        △PQR is isosceles ↔ P, Q, R form a triangle and
              (|PQ| = |PR| or |QP| = |QR| or |RP| = |RQ|)
  metamath    biid, since the notation's target is the triangle and the
              three equalities it chooses among
  target      biid
  note        Two sides equal, whichever two: a triangle named by its three
              points has no apex until a proof says which equality holds.

mundane definition equilateral
  let P be a point                                                    (H1)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H2)
  let R be a point                                                    (H3)
  then        △PQR is equilateral ↔ P, Q, R form a triangle and |PQ| = |QR|
              and |QR| = |RP|
  metamath    biid, since the notation's target is the triangle and the two
              equalities
  target      biid

mundane definition right-angled
  let P be a point                                                    (H1)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H2)
  let R be a point                                                    (H3)
  let V be a point                                                    (H4)
  then        △PQR is right-angled at V ↔ P, Q, R form a triangle and
              ((V = P and ∠QPR = 90°) or (V = Q and ∠PQR = 90°) or
              (V = R and ∠PRQ = 90°))
  metamath    biid, since the notation's target is the triangle and the
              right angle at whichever vertex V is
  target      biid
  note        V is one of the three vertices and the angle there is right.

mundane theorem right-angled-at-vertex
  let P be a point                                                    (H1)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H2)
  let R be a point                                                    (H3)
  then        △PQR is right-angled at R ↔ P, Q, R form a triangle and
              ∠PRQ = 90°
  metamath    grightat3
  target      grightat3
  note        Named at the vertex it writes last, a right angle is the angle
              there: the triangle's R ≠ P and R ≠ Q rule out the other two.

mundane theorem distance-symmetric
  let P be a point                                                    (H1)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H2)
  then        |PQ| = |QP|
  metamath    abssub
  target      abssub

mundane theorem angle-symmetric
  let P be a point                                                    (H1)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H2)
  let R be a point                                                    (H3)
  assume P, Q, R form a triangle                                      (H4)
  then        ∠PQR = ∠RQP
  metamath    gangsym3, which this corpus proves in elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm.
              The angle it writes is unsigned, which is what makes this true:
              ang itself is signed, and the two directions differ in sign. Off
              the branch cut that is arginv; on the cut nothing is negated and
              lognegb gives both directions as π. So the proof is in two cases
              and the absolute values agree either way.
  target      gangsym3

mundane theorem angle-turned
  let P be a point                                                    (H1)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H2)
  let R be a point                                                    (H3)
  assume P ≠ Q                                                        (H4)
  assume R ≠ Q                                                        (H5)
  then        ∠PQR = ∠RQP
  metamath    gangsym3, which this corpus proves in elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm
  target      gangsym3
  note        As angle-symmetric, for points that may lie on a line: the
              angle between two arms does not depend on which is named first.

mundane definition parallel
  open        As mun:stdlib/geometry/collinear is: in the plane PQ ∥ RS says
              (Q − P)/(S − R) is real, and the readable layer cannot subtract
              points. The notation carries a target, so a claim of
              parallelism elaborates; what has no readable statement is the
              equivalence.
  note        Named for the angle sum, whose proof draws the parallel to one
              side through the opposite vertex.

mundane definition between
  open        In the plane R is between S and T says S ≠ R and
              (T − R)/(S − R) is a negative real: T lies beyond R from S, on
              the same line. The readable layer cannot state it, as for
              mun:stdlib/geometry/collinear.

mundane definition on-line
  let P be a point                                                    (H1)
  let A be a point                                                    (H2)
  let B be a point                                                    (H3)
  then        P is on line AB ↔ A, B, P are collinear
  metamath    biid, since both notations' targets are the one quotient
  target      biid

mundane definition perpendicular
  open        In the plane line AB ⊥ line CD says A ≠ B, C ≠ D and
              (D − C)/(B − A) has real part 0: the two directions are at a
              right angle. The readable layer cannot state it, as for
              mun:stdlib/geometry/parallel; where the lines meet,
              mun:stdlib/geometry/perpendicular-by-right-angle reads it as
              an angle.

theorem perpendicular-by-right-angle
  let P be a point                                                    (H1)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H2)
  let R be a point                                                    (H3)
  assume P ≠ Q                                                        (H4)
  assume R ≠ Q                                                        (H5)
  then        line QP ⊥ line QR ↔ ∠PQR = 90°
  metamath    gperp90, from angrteqvd
  target      gperp90
  note        Two lines through Q are perpendicular when the angle between
              them at Q is a right angle.

theorem intersecting-chords
  let A be a point                                                    (H1)
  let B be a point                                                    (H2)
  let C be a point                                                    (H3)
  let D be a point                                                    (H4)
  let P be a point                                                    (H5)
  let O be a point                                                    (H6)
  assume P is between A and B                                         (H7)
  assume P is between C and D                                         (H8)
  assume |AO| = |BO|                                                  (H9)
  assume |AO| = |CO|                                                  (H10)
  assume |AO| = |DO|                                                  (H11)
  then        |PA|·|PB| = |PC|·|PD|
  metamath    gchord, from chordthm
  target      gchord
  note        Two chords AB and CD of a circle with centre O, crossing at P,
              cut each other into parts with equal products (Euclid III.35).
              The circle is said by the four distances to O.

theorem chord-midpoint-right-angle
  let A be a point                                                    (H1)
  let B be a point                                                    (H2)
  let M be a point                                                    (H3)
  let O be a point                                                    (H4)
  assume M is the midpoint of AB                                      (H5)
  assume |AO| = |BO|                                                  (H6)
  assume O ≠ M                                                        (H7)
  then        ∠OMB = 90°
  metamath    gchordmid, from chordthmlem
  target      gchordmid
  note        The line from the centre O of a circle through the midpoint M
              of a chord AB meets the chord at a right angle (Euclid III.3).
              O ≠ M because a chord through the centre has its midpoint
              there, and no angle at M.

mundane definition midpoint
  let M be a point                                                    (H1)
  let A be a point                                                    (H2)
  let B be a point                                                    (H3)
  then        M is the midpoint of AB ↔ M is between A and B and |AM| = |MB|
  metamath    biid, since the notation's target is the betweenness and the
              equal halves
  target      biid
  note        In the plane the midpoint is (A + B)/2, which the page does not
              write: it adds points.

mundane theorem midpoint-unique
  let A be a point                                                    (H1)
  let B be a point                                                    (H2)
  let M be a point                                                    (H3)
  let N be a point                                                    (H4)
  assume M is the midpoint of AB                                      (H5)
  assume N is the midpoint of AB                                      (H6)
  then        M = N
  metamath    gmiduniq, which this corpus proves in
              elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm from gmidval: each is (A + B)/2
  target      gmiduniq
  note        A segment has one midpoint, which is what "the midpoint of AB"
              takes for granted.

mundane theorem midpoint-determines-end
  let A be a point                                                    (H1)
  let B be a point                                                    (H2)
  let C be a point                                                    (H3)
  let M be a point                                                    (H4)
  assume M is the midpoint of AB                                      (H5)
  assume M is the midpoint of AC                                      (H6)
  then        B = C
  metamath    gmidend, which this corpus proves in
              elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm from gmidval: (A + B)/2 =
              (A + C)/2 cancels to B = C
  target      gmidend
  note        The reflection of a point through another is one point: B is
              the point beyond M from A at the distance |AM|.

mundane definition same-side
  open        Which side of the line through P and Q a point X is on is the
              sign of the imaginary part of (X − P)/(Q − P): positive on one
              side, negative on the other, zero on the line. Two points are on
              the same side when the two signs multiply to a positive number,
              and on opposite sides when to a negative one. The readable
              layer cannot state it, as for mun:stdlib/geometry/collinear.
  note        One record for both notations, same-side and opposite-sides,
              since they are one sign read two ways.

axiom parallel-through
  let P be a point                                                    (H1)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H2)
  let R be a point                                                    (H3)
  assume P, Q, R form a triangle                                      (H4)
  then        there are D, E ∈ 𝔼² with line RD ∥ line PQ, line RE ∥ line QP,
              R is between D and E, D and Q are on opposite sides of line RP,
              E and P are on opposite sides of line RQ, and P and Q are on the
              same side of line RD
  metamath    gparthru, which this corpus proves in elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm:
              set.mm has no parallels to state it with
  target      gparthru
  note        The line through R parallel to PQ, with a point on it each side
              of R: D on P's side and E on Q's. What a figure shows of that
              line is written out, since nothing else can supply it. An axiom
              in Euclid, as his fifth postulate, and in Hilbert, as the
              parallel axiom; over ℂ it is a theorem, with D = R + (P − Q) and
              E = R + (Q − P).

theorem alternate-angles
  let P be a point                                                    (H1)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H2)
  let R be a point                                                    (H3)
  let S be a point                                                    (H4)
  assume P, Q, R form a triangle                                      (H5)
  assume line RS ∥ line PQ                                            (H6)
  assume S and Q are on opposite sides of line RP                     (H7)
  then        ∠SRP = ∠RPQ
  metamath    galtang, which this corpus proves in elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm:
              set.mm has no parallels to state it with
  target      galtang
  note        The line RP crosses the two parallels RS and PQ, and the angles
              it makes with them on opposite sides are equal. Over ℂ, S − R
              is a negative multiple of Q − P, because S and Q are on opposite
              sides, and the two angles are one angle read from each end.

theorem angles-on-a-line
  let P be a point                                                    (H1)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H2)
  let R be a point                                                    (H3)
  let S be a point                                                    (H4)
  let T be a point                                                    (H5)
  assume R is between S and T                                         (H6)
  assume P and Q are on the same side of line RS                      (H7)
  assume S and Q are on opposite sides of line RP                     (H8)
  then        ∠SRP + ∠PRQ + ∠TRQ = 180°
  metamath    gline, which this corpus proves in elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm:
              set.mm has no sides of a line to state it with
  target      gline
  note        The three angles at R between S, P, Q and T, in that order,
              make up the straight line ST. H7 puts P and Q on one side of
              the line and H8 puts P between S and Q as seen from R, which is
              the order. Over ℂ the three angles turn the same way, and their
              signed sum is half a turn.

theorem supplementary-angles
  let A be a point                                                    (H1)
  let B be a point                                                    (H2)
  let C be a point                                                    (H3)
  let D be a point                                                    (H4)
  assume B is between A and C                                         (H5)
  assume D ≠ B                                                        (H6)
  then        ∠ABD + ∠DBC = 180°
  metamath    gsupp, which this corpus proves in elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm
              from cosangneg2d, cospim and cos11
  target      gsupp
  note        Two angles on a line, Euclid I.13: with B on the segment AC,
              the angles ABD and DBC make a straight angle wherever D is.

theorem triangle-inequality-points
  let P be a point                                                    (H1)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H2)
  let R be a point                                                    (H3)
  then        |PR| ≤ |PQ| + |QR|
  metamath    abs3dif
  target      abs3dif

theorem triangle-equality-between
  let P be a point                                                    (H1)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H2)
  let R be a point                                                    (H3)
  assume P ≠ Q                                                        (H4)
  assume Q ≠ R                                                        (H5)
  assume |PQ| + |QR| = |PR|                                           (H6)
  then        Q is between P and R
  metamath    gtrieq, which this corpus proves in elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm:
              set.mm has no equality case of the triangle inequality
  target      gtrieq
  note        Where the way through Q is no longer than the straight way,
              Q lies on the segment PR. Squared, the equality says the
              product of the differences from Q with one conjugated has its
              real part equal to minus its size, so it is a negative real.

theorem triangle-inequality-strict
  let P be a point                                                    (H1)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H2)
  let R be a point                                                    (H3)
  assume P, Q, R form a triangle                                      (H4)
  then        |PR| < |PQ| + |QR|
  metamath    gtristrict, which this corpus proves in
              elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm from gtrieq and abs3dif
  target      gtristrict
  note        Euclid I.20: equality would put Q between P and R, and the
              three would be collinear.

theorem angle-addition
  let A be a point                                                    (H1)
  let B be a point                                                    (H2)
  let C be a point                                                    (H3)
  let M be a point                                                    (H4)
  assume A, B, C form a triangle                                      (H5)
  assume M is between A and B                                         (H6)
  then        ∠ACM + ∠MCB = ∠ACB
  metamath    gangadd, which this corpus proves in
              elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm: set.mm adds no two angles
  target      gangadd
  note        A point of the side AB splits the angle at C in two. With T
              the point beyond C from A, angles-on-a-line makes ACM, MCB
              and BCT a straight angle, and supplementary-angles makes ACB
              and BCT one: M and B lie on one side of line CA, and A and B
              on two sides of line CM, which is what the line through M
              does.

mundane theorem distance-nonneg
  let P be a point                                                    (H1)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H2)
  then        |PQ| ≥ 0
  metamath    gdistnn, which this corpus proves in elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm
              from absge0
  target      gdistnn

mundane theorem distance-positive
  let P be a point                                                    (H1)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H2)
  assume P ≠ Q                                                        (H3)
  then        |PQ| > 0
  metamath    gdistpos, which this corpus proves in elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm
              from absgt0
  target      gdistpos

mundane theorem distance-zero
  let P be a point                                                    (H1)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H2)
  then        |PQ| = 0 ↔ P = Q
  metamath    gdist0, which this corpus proves in elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm
              from abs00 and subeq0
  target      gdist0
  note        A point is at distance 0 from itself and from no other point.

mundane theorem distance-by-coordinates
  let a ∈ ℝ                                                           (H1)
  let b ∈ ℝ                                                           (H2)
  let c ∈ ℝ                                                           (H3)
  let d ∈ ℝ                                                           (H4)
  then        |(a, b)(c, d)| = √((a − c)² + (b − d)²)
  metamath    gdistco, which this corpus proves in elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm
              from absreim
  target      gdistco

mundane theorem collinear-by-coordinates
  let a ∈ ℝ                                                           (H1)
  let b ∈ ℝ                                                           (H2)
  let c ∈ ℝ                                                           (H3)
  let d ∈ ℝ                                                           (H4)
  let e ∈ ℝ                                                           (H5)
  let f ∈ ℝ                                                           (H6)
  then        (a, b), (c, d), (e, f) are collinear ↔ (c − a)·(f − b) = (e − a)·(d − b)
  metamath    gcolco, which this corpus proves in elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm
              from gdivre: a quotient is real just where the first times
              the conjugate of the second has imaginary part 0
  target      gcolco

mundane theorem between-by-coordinates
  let a ∈ ℝ                                                           (H1)
  let b ∈ ℝ                                                           (H2)
  let c ∈ ℝ                                                           (H3)
  let d ∈ ℝ                                                           (H4)
  let e ∈ ℝ                                                           (H5)
  let f ∈ ℝ                                                           (H6)
  then        (c, d) is between (a, b) and (e, f) ↔ (a − c)·(f − d) = (e − c)·(b − d) and (a − c)·(e − c) + (b − d)·(f − d) < 0
  metamath    gbetco, which this corpus proves in elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm
              from gdivneg: a quotient is a negative real just where the
              first times the conjugate of the second has imaginary part 0
              and negative real part
  target      gbetco
  note        The three points are collinear, and the two ends lie on
              opposite sides of the middle one: the differences from it
              point opposite ways, so their dot product is negative.

mundane theorem points-apart-by-coordinates
  let A be a point                                                    (H1)
  let B be a point                                                    (H2)
  let a ∈ ℝ                                                           (H3)
  let b ∈ ℝ                                                           (H4)
  let c ∈ ℝ                                                           (H5)
  let d ∈ ℝ                                                           (H6)
  assume A = (a, b)                                                   (H7)
  assume B = (c, d)                                                   (H8)
  then        A ≠ B ↔ (a, b) ≠ (c, d)
  metamath    gptapart, which this corpus proves in
              elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm from cru and opthneg: each
              differs just where a coordinate does
  target      gptapart
  note        On the left (a, b) and (c, d) are points, as A and B are;
              on the right they are pairs, which `arithmetic` compares
              coordinate by coordinate (`METHODS.md`, Pairs).

mundane theorem distance-of-points-by-coordinates
  let A be a point                                                    (H1)
  let B be a point                                                    (H2)
  let a ∈ ℝ                                                           (H3)
  let b ∈ ℝ                                                           (H4)
  let c ∈ ℝ                                                           (H5)
  let d ∈ ℝ                                                           (H6)
  assume A = (a, b)                                                   (H7)
  assume B = (c, d)                                                   (H8)
  then        |AB| = √((a − c)² + (b − d)²)
  metamath    gptdist, which this corpus proves in
              elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm from gdistco, the points put for
              their coordinates
  target      gptdist
  note        distance-by-coordinates for two points named by letters, so a
              proof that gives its points coordinates reads each distance
              off in one step.

mundane theorem between-symmetric
  let R be a point                                                    (H1)
  let S be a point                                                    (H2)
  let T be a point                                                    (H3)
  assume R is between S and T                                         (H4)
  then        R is between T and S
  metamath    gbetsym, which this corpus proves in elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm:
              the quotient read the other way is its reciprocal
  target      gbetsym

mundane theorem between-apart
  let R be a point                                                    (H1)
  let S be a point                                                    (H2)
  let T be a point                                                    (H3)
  assume R is between S and T                                         (H4)
  then        S ≠ R. T ≠ R.
  metamath    gbetne0, which this corpus proves in
              elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm: the first is part of what
              between says, and T = R would make the quotient 0, which
              is not negative
  target      gbetne0
  note        A point between two others is apart from both, so the
              angles and triangles at it are defined.

mundane theorem between-collinear
  let R be a point                                                    (H1)
  let S be a point                                                    (H2)
  let T be a point                                                    (H3)
  assume R is between S and T                                         (H4)
  then        T, R, S are collinear
  metamath    gbetcol, which this corpus proves in
              elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm from gtricol
  target      gbetcol
  note        A point between two others is on their line.

mundane theorem angle-along-segment
  let P be a point                                                    (H1)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H2)
  let R be a point                                                    (H3)
  let S be a point                                                    (H4)
  assume S is between P and Q                                         (H5)
  assume R ≠ P                                                        (H6)
  then        ∠SPR = ∠QPR
  metamath    gangseg, which this corpus proves in elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm
  target      gangseg
  note        S on the segment from P to Q is on the ray from P through Q,
              so the angle that ray makes with PR is the same measured to S
              as to Q.

theorem segment-addition
  let P be a point                                                    (H1)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H2)
  let S be a point                                                    (H3)
  assume S is between P and Q                                         (H4)
  then        |PS| + |SQ| = |PQ|
  metamath    gsegadd, which this corpus proves in elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm
  target      gsegadd

mundane theorem sine-positive
  let P be a point                                                    (H1)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H2)
  let R be a point                                                    (H3)
  assume P, Q, R form a triangle                                      (H4)
  then        sin(∠PQR) > 0
  metamath    gsinpos, which this corpus proves in elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm
  target      gsinpos
  note        An angle of a triangle is strictly between 0° and 180°, where
              the sine is positive.

theorem law-of-sines
  let P be a point                                                    (H1)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H2)
  let R be a point                                                    (H3)
  assume P, Q, R form a triangle                                      (H4)
  then        |PQ|·sin(∠PQR) = |PR|·sin(∠QRP)
  metamath    glawsin, which this corpus proves in elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm
              from heron, taken at Q and at R
  target      glawsin
  note        Each side over the sine of the angle opposite it is the same:
              PR is opposite Q and PQ opposite R, so |PR|/sin(∠PQR) =
              |PQ|/sin(∠QRP), written without division.

mundane definition area
  let P be a point                                                    (H1)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H2)
  let R be a point                                                    (H3)
  then        the area of △PQR = (1/2)·(|QR|·|PR|)·|sin(∡QRP)|
  metamath    eqid, since the notation's target is the right side
  target      eqid
  note        Half of two sides times the sine of the angle between them,
              the sine's size taken so that the area does not depend on which
              way the triangle runs.

theorem area-by-sine
  let P be a point                                                    (H1)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H2)
  let R be a point                                                    (H3)
  assume P ≠ R                                                        (H4)
  assume Q ≠ R                                                        (H5)
  then        the area of △PQR = (1/2)·(|QR|·|PR|)·sin(∠QRP)
  metamath    garea, from gsinabs
  target      garea

mundane theorem area-positive
  let P be a point                                                    (H1)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H2)
  let R be a point                                                    (H3)
  assume P, Q, R form a triangle                                      (H4)
  then        the area of △PQR > 0
  metamath    gareapos, from gsinpos
  target      gareapos

theorem heron
  let P be a point                                                    (H1)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H2)
  let R be a point                                                    (H3)
  let s ∈ ℝ                                                           (H4)
  assume P ≠ R                                                        (H5)
  assume Q ≠ R                                                        (H6)
  assume s = (|QR| + |PR| + |PQ|)/2                                   (H7)
  then        the area of △PQR = √(s·(s − |QR|)·((s − |PR|)·(s − |PQ|)))
  metamath    gheron, from heron
  target      gheron
  note        Heron's formula: the area from the three sides alone, s being
              half the perimeter.

theorem law-of-cosines
  let P be a point                                                    (H1)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H2)
  let R be a point                                                    (H3)
  assume P ≠ Q                                                        (H4)
  assume R ≠ Q                                                        (H5)
  then        |RP|² = |PQ|² + |QR|² − 2·(|PQ|·|QR|·cos(∠PQR))
  metamath    glawcos, which this corpus proves in elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm
              from lawcos
  target      glawcos
  note        The side opposite Q, from the two sides at Q and the angle
              between them; at a right angle the last term is 0 and it is
              Pythagoras.

mundane theorem angle-equal-by-cosine
  let P be a point                                                    (H1)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H2)
  let R be a point                                                    (H3)
  let P′ be a point                                                   (H4)
  let Q′ be a point                                                   (H5)
  let R′ be a point                                                   (H6)
  assume P ≠ Q                                                        (H7)
  assume R ≠ Q                                                        (H8)
  assume P′ ≠ Q′                                                      (H9)
  assume R′ ≠ Q′                                                      (H10)
  assume cos(∠PQR) = cos(∠P′Q′R′)                                     (H11)
  then        ∠PQR = ∠P′Q′R′
  metamath    gangeq3, from cos11
  target      gangeq3
  note        An angle is between 0° and 180°, where the cosine is one-to-one.

mundane theorem angle-order-by-cosine
  let P be a point                                                    (H1)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H2)
  let R be a point                                                    (H3)
  let P′ be a point                                                   (H4)
  let Q′ be a point                                                   (H5)
  let R′ be a point                                                   (H6)
  assume P ≠ Q                                                        (H7)
  assume R ≠ Q                                                        (H8)
  assume P′ ≠ Q′                                                      (H9)
  assume R′ ≠ Q′                                                      (H10)
  then        ∠PQR < ∠P′Q′R′ ↔ cos(∠P′Q′R′) < cos(∠PQR)
  metamath    gangord3, from cosord
  target      gangord3
  note        An angle is between 0° and 180°, where the cosine falls: the
              larger angle has the smaller cosine.

mundane theorem right-angle-by-cosine
  let P be a point                                                    (H1)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H2)
  let R be a point                                                    (H3)
  assume P ≠ Q                                                        (H4)
  assume R ≠ Q                                                        (H5)
  then        ∠PQR = 90° ↔ cos(∠PQR) = 0
  metamath    gright3, from coseq00topi
  target      gright3

mundane theorem angle-at-most-straight
  let P be a point                                                    (H1)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H2)
  let R be a point                                                    (H3)
  assume P ≠ Q                                                        (H4)
  assume R ≠ Q                                                        (H5)
  then        ∠PQR ≤ 180°
  metamath    gangle180, which this corpus proves in
              elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm from gangicc3
  target      gangle180

mundane theorem angle-by-cosine
  let P be a point                                                    (H1)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H2)
  let R be a point                                                    (H3)
  let x ∈ ℝ                                                           (H4)
  assume P ≠ Q                                                        (H5)
  assume R ≠ Q                                                        (H6)
  assume 0 ≤ x                                                        (H7)
  assume x ≤ 180°                                                     (H8)
  then        ∠PQR = x ↔ cos(∠PQR) = cos(x)
  metamath    gangcos, which this corpus proves in elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm
              from gangicc3 and cos11
  target      gangcos
  note        An angle is read from its cosine, as right-angle-by-cosine
              reads a right angle: on [0°, 180°] the cosine takes each value
              once.

theorem altitude-foot
  let P be a point                                                    (H1)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H2)
  let R be a point                                                    (H3)
  assume P, Q, R form a triangle                                      (H4)
  assume ∠PRQ = 90°                                                   (H5)
  then        there is D ∈ 𝔼² with D is between P and Q, ∠PDR = 90°,
              ∠QDR = 90°, P, D, R form a triangle, and Q, D, R form a
              triangle
  metamath    galtfoot, which this corpus proves in elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm:
              D is P + t(Q − P), with t the real part of (R − P)/(Q − P)
  target      galtfoot
  note        The perpendicular from the right angle meets the hypotenuse
              between its ends, and splits the triangle into two right
              triangles.

axiom side-angle-side
  let P be a point                                                    (H1)
  let Q be a point                                                    (H2)
  let R be a point                                                    (H3)
  let P′ be a point                                                   (H4)
  let Q′ be a point                                                   (H5)
  let R′ be a point                                                   (H6)
  assume P, Q, R form a triangle                                      (H7)
  assume P′, Q′, R′ form a triangle                                   (H8)
  assume |PQ| = |P′Q′|                                                (H9)
  assume ∠PQR = ∠P′Q′R′                                               (H10)
  assume |QR| = |Q′R′|                                                (H11)
  then        triangle PQR ≅ triangle P′Q′R′
  metamath    gsas, which this corpus proves in elaboration/stdlib/proved.mm. An
              axiom in Euclid and in Hilbert, because a synthetic geometry has
              no coordinates to compute with and congruence has to be
              stipulated; over ℂ it is a theorem. The proof is the law of
              cosines at each of the three vertices, six applications in all,
              which are three cyclic rotations of glawcos in each triangle.
              The third side comes first, since equal sides and equal cosines
              make equal squares and a length is not negative; with all three
              sides equal the other two angles follow the other way round.
  target      gsas
  note        The isosceles proof instantiates A, B, C, A′, B′, C′, which are not
              this statement's variable names. One of the two must change; the
              merge changed neither. See DATABASE.md.
